Blind image deblinking aims to jointly estimate the blur kernel and the latent clean image from a single blurred observation. However, this task is frequently challenged by the vast and complex solution space, which hinders reliable convergence for many models. We propose an innovative method that reimagines deblinking as a priors-based classification task, thereby avoiding the need for joint optimization. Our approach is simple yet robust, ensuring consistent performance across different datasets and benchmark tests. Specifically, we model the distribution of clean images to learn a classifier that reconstructs a clean image from latent features. These features are produced by a deblinking network trained on a set of paired images; in our setup, paired features are assigned to their respective clean images to learn the clean image distribution. Consequently, we achieve a consistent feature representation that simplifies the classification task. We offer a detailed assessment with both quantitative metrics and qualitative analysis. Our method consistently outperforms current state-of-the-art approaches while achieving remarkable qualitative results. The code is available at https://github.com/ahmed-1996/DeepDeblur.
